+    def set_error_reporter(self, reporter: ErrorReporter[_ReqT] | None) -> None:
+        """Set a callable to be notified of exceptions raised by the app.
+
+        The reporter is called for every exception that is passed to the
+        app's error handling, regardless of whether it was raised while
+        routing the request, in a middleware component, hook or responder,
+        or while rendering the response body. This includes instances of
+        :class:`~.HTTPError` and :class:`~.HTTPStatus` too (for example, a
+        request to an unknown route is reported as
+        :class:`~.HTTPRouteNotFound`).
+
+        This makes the reporter a convenient place to log exceptions, or to
+        send them to an error tracker, without having to replace or wrap the
+        error handlers themselves::
+
+            def report_error(req, ex, params, handled):
+                if not isinstance(ex, (falcon.HTTPError, falcon.HTTPStatus)):
+                    logging.error(
+                        'Error serving %s %s', req.method, req.path, exc_info=ex
+                    )
+
+            app.set_error_reporter(report_error)
+
+        The reporter is called before the matching error handler (see also:
+        :meth:`~.add_error_handler`) is invoked, and it does not alter the
+        way the exception is handled. In addition to the above, the
+        following exceptions are reported:
+
+        * If an error handler raises an instance of :class:`~.HTTPError` or
+          :class:`~.HTTPStatus`, that exception is reported as handled too,
+          and the response is rendered from it as usual. (However, if the
+          handler reraises the very same exception object that it was passed,
+          the exception is not reported a second time.)
+        * If an error handler, or the error serializer (see also:
+          :meth:`~.set_error_serializer`), raises any other exception, it is
+          reported as unhandled, and propagates to the application server.
+        * If the response cannot be started (for instance, in the case
+          `resp.status` was set to an invalid value), the resulting exception
+          is reported as unhandled, and propagates to the application server.
+
+        Exceptions raised after the response has been started, such as while
+        iterating over a response stream, cannot be observed by the app, and
+        are thus not reported.
+
+        Note:
+            Only one reporter can be set at a time; calling this method again
+            replaces the previously set one. Pass ``None`` to remove the
